    {
      "id": "1000",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1000,
        "body_ja": "",
        "body": "<p>い is a sentence-ending particle in Japanese that will often be seen in compound structures with other particles, or auxiliary verbs. It adds a sense of familiarity and friendliness to the base meaning of what it is being paired with.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>い is usually only used by men, when speaking to someone of a lower social position. This social position difference is more in reference to age than anything else, so い being used when an elderly person is speaking to a younger person is very common.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This feeling of familiarity is best conveyed when い is paired with the sentence-ending particle か, or the auxiliary verb だ.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n <li data-study-question='155293'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155294'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>い may also be paired with more emphatic sentence-ending particles such as わ, や, and ぞ, or the auxiliary verb た. However, these combinations are far less common, and won't be seen very often.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n <li data-study-question='155295'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155296'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155297'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155298'></ul>\r\n\r\n<section class='caution'><h4>Caution</h4>\r\n<p>Despite い primarily being used by men, it is not a strict rule, and women do occasionally use it. This is especially true when partnered with either か, だ, or わ.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li class='writeup-example'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184635'></ul></section>\r\n\r\n<section class='caution'><h4>Caution</h4>\r\n<p>Although uncommon, い can also be used with the imperative form of verbs to create a slightly less strict sounding order or command.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184636'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184637'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Once again, this use is very rare, and will almost never actually be used in real life. It may, however, appear in books.</p></section>"
      }
    }
